Strategy selection is implemented via the LVLM following five ordered principles, which together enforce a privacy-utility trade-off: (1) start small , where we prioritize clues by c ∗ = arg ⁡ max c i ⁡ s i / | r i | c^{*}=\arg\max_{c_{i}}s_{i}/|r_{i}| , favoring those with a high ranking score s i s_{i} relative to their region area r i r_{i} ;
(2) blend naturally , where all modifications specified by the generated prompt ρ \rho must be consistent with the scene’s lighting, perspective, and style;
(3) stay culturally close , where replacements must be drawn from a geographically adjacent region to remain visually plausible;
(4) preserve the main subject , where r ∗ r^{*} must not overlap with the primary subject region detected by the LVLM;
(5) persist and escalate , where upon failure, retry within σ \sigma with a revised ρ \rho , then switches to an alternative strategy σ ′ ∈ Σ ∖ { σ } \sigma^{\prime}\in\Sigma\setminus\{\sigma\} .
